WEBVTT

NOTE
A diarised, timed discovery call. Every other example in this directory is
untimed plain text, which means conversation dynamics (talk time in seconds,
interruptions, response latency, speech rate) report as unavailable for all of
them. This one exercises that path end to end.

Timings are deliberately realistic rather than tidy: the rep talks over the
prospect once at 02:08, the prospect leaves a long think-pause at 01:14, and
the prospect's share of the conversation climbs as the call goes on.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.400
<v Rep>Hi Priya, thanks for making the time. I know calendars are brutal this
time of year, so I'll keep this useful or let you go early.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.100
<v Prospect>No problem. I've got about twenty minutes.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:24.900
<v Rep>Perfect. I'd rather not pitch at you, so can I start with how your team
handles ingestion pipelines today? Just the honest version, not the architecture
diagram version.

4
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:47.200
<v Prospect>Honestly it's held together with tape. We've got about eighty
pipelines, all cron, and two engineers who spend most of their week babysitting
them. Last month we had a silent failure that cost us three days of revenue
reporting before anyone noticed.

5
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.600
<v Rep>Three days. What did that cost you to unpick?

6
00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:12.800
<v Prospect>Two engineers for most of a week, plus the finance team redid their
close. I'd put it somewhere north of forty thousand once you count the
distraction. That's the part that got leadership's attention.

7
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.100
<v Rep>And is that attention attached to a budget?

8
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:49.700
<v Prospect>It is, actually. We've got allocation approved for data tooling
this quarter — I think it's around sixty thousand. I sign off up to fifty
myself, anything above that needs my VP, but she's the one who asked me to go
looking.

9
00:01:50.300 --> 00:02:00.100
<v Rep>That's helpful. What's the deadline driving it? You said this quarter.

10
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:14.600
<v Prospect>We're going live with a new product in March, and the current setup
will not survive the volume. So we need something in place by end of February,
realistically, which means deciding in the next few weeks.

11
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:16.200
<v Rep>Right, so February is the hard date.

12
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:31.400
<v Prospect>February is the hard date. I should say we're also looking at one
other vendor, so this isn't a sole-source thing. But your integration story is
the reason you're on the list at all.

13
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:44.800
<v Rep>I appreciate the straightness. What would you need to see from us to be
comfortable recommending it to your VP?

14
00:02:45.600 --> 00:03:18.900
<v Prospect>A working proof of concept against two of our ugliest pipelines,
and some answer on SOC 2 because our security review will ask. If it handles
those two without me writing glue code, that's most of the argument. The other
vendor wanted six weeks for a POC, which doesn't fit February at all.

15
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:30.200
<v Rep>We can do a POC in a week on your real pipelines, and I'll send the SOC 2
report over today. Shall we put Tuesday in the calendar to kick it off?

16
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:40.600
<v Prospect>Tuesday works. Send the invite to me and I'll add my lead
engineer — he's the one who'll actually try to break it.
